From ee117a3ed91de7b45896250dc6910107398cfdc6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:36:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(interaction): predict held trapdoor placement

Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5 keeps the first predicted trapdoor facing, half
and world hit during held placement. Preserve that state across trapdoor
materials, temporary item-family changes and server correction. Apply the
snap only while holding a trapdoor. Other block items use fresh hits while
suppressing repeated activation; an empty hand restores native interaction.
Returning to a trapdoor reuses the original snap until physical release.

Carry the use lifetime and sequence-owned prediction format 2 through
direct and ViaProxy sessions. Keep the native cursor separate from the
ordinary Java raycast packet, including coordinates outside later target
cubes. Preserve independent water, power and material properties. Separate
trapdoors and the original support remain eligible while snapped.

Match the 300 ms delay after the first attempt or a successful use. Failed
repeats do not restart it. Poll subsequent attempts from the input frame
after camera movement, before the next game tick publishes rotation.
Suppress Java's parallel four-tick repeat path for that active hold.
This preserves native placement/camera ordering without forcing server
orientation. Release, screens and connection replacement retire the
input timer and placement state.

Keep the existing native outline. Fresh native bottom-face hits use
0.8175; the earlier 0.8125 claim came from the Java fixture. Clock-boundary,
item-state and block-state tests cover the retained input state. Private
native, direct and proxy captures establish gameplay behavior separately
from these model tests.

Select the existing native outline and occlusion behavior from the active
Bedrock connection, including remote ViaProxy sessions. Their local Java
target version does not identify the backend. Without this check fresh
proxy hits retained Java's 0.8125 bottom instead of native 0.8175.
